# Sprint 1 Journal — Project Setup & Core Backend

## Objective

Set up the Smart Hospital Queue Optimizer and establish the core backend
architecture.

## Work Completed

- Created the Spring Boot project.
- Configured the Maven dependencies.
- Established the backend package structure.
- Implemented the Doctor entity.
- Implemented the Department entity.
- Implemented the User entity.
- Implemented the Token entity.
- Created the initial repositories, services, and controllers.
- Created and tested the basic REST API structure.

## Challenges

Initially, the main challenge was organizing the backend structure so that
entities, repositories, services, and controllers remained separated and
easy to maintain.

## Resolution

The backend was organized into separate layers, allowing database access,
business logic, and API handling to remain independent.

## Outcome

The core Spring Boot backend was successfully established and the main
entities required for hospital queue management were implemented.

## Next Steps

The next sprint focused on implementing the complete token and queue
lifecycle.


# Sprint 2 Journal — Queue Management

## Objective

The main objective of Sprint 2 was to implement the core hospital queue
management functionality and establish the complete lifecycle of a patient
token.

## Work Completed

- Implemented token creation and management.
- Connected tokens with the appropriate doctor and department.
- Implemented queue ordering and management.
- Added different token/queue states.
- Implemented the complete queue lifecycle from token creation to completion.
- Added backend APIs for performing queue-related operations.
- Tested the queue operations through the backend.

## Technical Work

The queue management functionality was implemented in the Spring Boot
backend. The Token entity was integrated with the other core entities so
that tokens could be associated with doctors and departments.

The queue was designed to maintain patients in their appropriate order.
Tokens can move through the required stages of the queue lifecycle, allowing
the system to track the current state of each patient.

## Challenges Faced

One of the main challenges was ensuring that token states and queue ordering
were handled correctly during different queue operations.

Another challenge was maintaining the relationship between tokens, doctors,
and departments while performing queue operations.

## Solution

The queue operations were implemented through separate backend services and
REST APIs. The queue state was updated whenever an operation was performed,
allowing the system to maintain the current status of each token.

## Outcome

By the end of Sprint 2, the system had a functional queue management
component with a complete token lifecycle.

The backend could create and manage tokens and process patients through the
hospital queue.

## Next Sprint

The next sprint focused on implementing formula-based waiting-time
estimation and integrating PostgreSQL for permanent data persistence.


# Sprint 3 Journal — PostgreSQL & Waiting-Time Estimation

## Objective

The main objective of Sprint 3 was to implement the waiting-time estimation
functionality and replace temporary data storage with persistent PostgreSQL
storage.

## Work Completed

- Implemented formula-based waiting-time estimation.
- Integrated waiting-time calculation with the active queue.
- Configured PostgreSQL as the project database.
- Connected the Spring Boot application to PostgreSQL.
- Persisted users, doctors, departments, and tokens in the database.
- Tested database operations.
- Verified that data remains available after application restarts.

## Waiting-Time Estimation

The system uses a simple formula-based approach rather than a machine
learning model.

The estimated waiting time is calculated using:

**Estimated Wait Time = Patients Ahead × Average Consultation Time**

The number of active patients ahead of a patient is determined from the
current queue, and the average consultation time is used to estimate the
patient's expected waiting period.

## Database Implementation

PostgreSQL was integrated with the Spring Boot backend to provide permanent
data persistence.

Previously, application data could be lost when the application was
restarted. After integrating PostgreSQL, important application data is
stored in the database and remains available after restarts.

## Challenges Faced

The main challenges were configuring the PostgreSQL connection correctly and
ensuring that the application entities and queue operations worked correctly
with persistent database storage.

Another challenge was making sure that the waiting-time calculation used
the current queue state rather than static or temporary information.

## Solution

The Spring Boot application was configured to communicate with PostgreSQL,
and the existing entities were connected to persistent database storage.

The waiting-time calculation was integrated with the queue so that the
estimated waiting time could be calculated based on the patients currently
ahead in the queue.

## Testing

The team tested:

- Token creation.
- Queue operations.
- Database insertion and retrieval.
- Waiting-time calculation.
- Data persistence after application restart.
- Interaction between queue management and waiting-time estimation.

## Outcome

By the end of Sprint 3, PostgreSQL persistence was successfully implemented
and the application data survived restarts.

The waiting-time estimation functionality was also integrated with the
queue, providing an estimated waiting time based on the number of patients
ahead and the average consultation time.

## Next Sprint

The next sprint focused on implementing user authentication, password
hashing, JWT-based login, and authorization.
